Z||000026||Fformula88||10-29-2001||03:09 PM||||magazine numbers aren't necessarily comparable to what you run at the track.  I know Motor Trend (and probably all the others) adjust there times to a specific atmospheric condition so they are comparable, wherever and whenever they are tested.  Since a time slip does not take into account wind barometric preasure, altitude, temperature etc it is not directly comparable.<P>In other words a car that a magazine tested in the low 16's doesn't mean its impossible for it to run quicker.  You could have a Fiero run low 15's at one venue on a given day, and high 15's or low 16's at another venue a different day just because of atmospheric conditions.  So unless you take a Fiero, a Mustang, and a whatever else to the track the same day to see how they stack up, arguing over a half second in the 1/4 is almost pointless.<P>BTW, I think those are pretty good times for a first time and second time run down the track!  As you learn how to launch the car better, they will come down!||||reg||1
Z||000027||88formula||10-29-2001||05:15 PM||||My 3.4 is an over head valve engine plucked from a Camaro. <P>Lou_dias Wednesday is the last day of the year to bring your car to New England Dragway. After Wednesday they are closed for the season. They are having what they call the Rock 101 street night. It’s basically for amateur drag racers with stock cars and slicks are not allowed that day. Do you want to bring your car down that day and we could meet there? I believe you have a 3.4-liter engine right?<P>Looking for a 14.5 @ 95 MPH.<P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 10-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000028||banditbalz||10-29-2001||08:25 PM||||Topic:   Went to the track today with the 3.4 engine. <P>We are getting a little off topic here.. If anyone wants to know more about MY quartermile times, launches or shifting techniques, I suggest you eithor use PM or start a new topic.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Silver 86 GT<BR>completely stock,<BR>completely fast!||||reg||1
Z||000029||AutoXdbdragSE||10-29-2001||11:36 PM||||i know a few years ago popular mechanics ran a test of 13 cars to see what was the fastest production 1/4 mile car in america, and they tested a bone stock fivespeed firebird.. base model lt1 w/ no power options.  for some reason runing stock rubber.. it ran the fastest times ever recorder for a stock firebird of any year, any engine.. including the 3.8 turbo.  they where mystified, the only explination they have is that sometimes engine tolerances line up and build a very fast car.. if you dyno run 4 randomly selected new of the lot cars you can get hp numbers that vary by as much as 20-40hp.. just in tollerances. it's strange but things happen.. and they happen for all cars.. be it fieros or mustangs.. oh, and a non factory set of rubber does wonders to et's of any car.. cause all factory rubber kinda sux.<BR><A HREF="http://popularmechanics.com/automotive/new_cars_trucks/1999/7/comparison_test_13_quickest_cars/index.phtml" TARGET=_blank>here is the article, a must read</A><BR>the firebirds numbers<BR>0-30 2.08<BR>0-60 4.94<BR>1/4 13.15 @ 108.27<P><BR>------------------<BR>Bahh.. V-6, who needs it.. i got me some 4.10's<P>[URL <A HREF="http://www.sounddomain.com/id/dbdrag2" TARGET=_blank>SEE]http://www.sounddomain.com/id/dbdrag2]SEE</A>  MY CAR, 84 fiero hittin 147.5DBs[/URL]<p>[This message has been edited by AutoXdbdragSE (edited 10-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000030||Tina||10-30-2001||12:52 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 88formula:<BR><B> I went to the track today for the first time in my life to see how my car would do and to just have fun. It was a blast <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Addicting, isn't it.<P>Now stop worrying about all the things, you ..think.. you did wrong.<P>Just look at it as a lot of room for improvement. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>You did good.<P>Tina  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000031||Alex4mula||10-30-2001||10:50 AM||||Hey 88Formula;<BR>How do you measure if you are lean or not at WOT?<P>------------------<BR>    Alex4mula<BR>:) Red-Original Owner & White-1/94 with PM (:||||reg||5
Z||000032||lou_dias||10-30-2001||12:27 PM||||My 3.4 isn't ready yet...<BR>Maybe another week or two.||||reg||1
Z||000033||88formula||10-30-2001||04:50 PM||||I've been told that if the O2 sensor reads 800mv or less at WOT than the air fuel ratio is lean. Mine reads about 925mv at WOT, which seams to give me the best acceleration under WOT conditions. I'm guessing the air fuel ratio is about 13-1 to 12.5-1 at those O2 sensor voltages although I cannot say for sure.<P><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 10-30-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000034||Alex4mula||10-30-2001||08:26 PM||||88Formula;<BR>I suggest you to invest $50 on an AutoMeter A/F ratio gage. I bought one from Summit and it is the best investestment after the 3.4. It takes all the guess work out as long as the O2 is functioning ok.. A lot better than reading a voltage. <BR>925mV is not lean at all. The leanest I have run is around 850mV reading the voltage but that is still in the rich side. If you get under 800mV then you can say you are going lean (gage too). If you pump up the presure too much the injector spray pattern is affected and thus performance. I have the Marren 2.8 injectors too and haven't go lean. Get that gage before anything else to help you out. Let me know if you want the PN info & pic of it in the car. Installation is very easy. Just my sugestion  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR>    Alex4mula<BR>:) Red-Original Owner & White-1/94 with PM (:||||reg||1
Z||000035||grinthock||10-30-2001||08:55 PM||||Just gotta add my 2 cents.<P>First off all, BanditBalz, I live (for another week!) and have lived in the same building as him for months, we cruise around all the time, I have looked at this engine as much as I could, so have others, it doesn't have a cam, it would sound different, mine does and we BOTH agree you can tell it does.<P>His car is stock, EXCLUDING the K&N Filter but I see that as a moot point.<P>The fact the pulls good times is his SKILL, I WANT him to take out my 85GT when I get it finished because I know he could drop at LEAST 1 tenth off my times because of his driving skill level.<P>DO NOT discount the driver when you are talking about quarter mile times, I have seen BAD drivers drive a car then someone better and seen .8 seconds difference!<P>Bandit is NOT pulling your chains guys, stock fiero 15.2, keep in mind, his car is a DAILY DRIVER but it's SUPER MAINTAINED, all the regular oil changes, and he is constantly checking things, to make sure everything is ok.<P>Well he does less maint on his car than I do, but if you do enough you do less (you get what I mean)<P>Nice times, I would think 14.5 would be attainable with a 3.4, I look forward to your improvement.  My 87GT is getting some performance work done over the winter, parts are on the way shortly, <P>Bandit -- Look to get your butt kicked at cayuga next year, the first time you see my car will be at the christmas tree!<P>Slammed -- Ok I won't ever corner like you, but the that little quarter mile is mine<P><BR>Quoted from a local import driver<BR>"I live my life a quarter mile at a time, nothing else matters, not the insurance, not my girl, not my tires, for those 19 seconds or more, I'm free!"<P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fieroworld.com" TARGET=_blank>www.fieroworld.com</A>  The place to go for Fiero INFO! <BR>Home of my 87 GT and 85 GT<BR>Justin C.<BR>Engineer, Designer and<BR>Technical Trainer for Nortel Networks Business Products.||||reg||1
